## STEP 2 · WHY PAUL CANNOT CREATE POLICIES, AND FIX IT
**What the records say (RELAYED, to test, not to trust):** SM 3.16 has no user table of its own: sign-in is a Windows account on the VM, and the role is the local group it belongs to (`B106_STATUS.md:60-63`): `HPIPSC` = Administrator, all features · `HPIPSC_Analyst` = all features with limited operations · `HPIPSC_Operator` = devices, tasks and alerts, **no policy or system changes** · `HPIPSC_Guest` = Dashboard and Reports, view only. Paul's account `hpsm-sm\paul.waite` is a member of **`HPIPSC_Analyst` only** (RELAYED `B306_STATUS.md:52`; B106 read his sign-in role as Analyst, `B106_STATUS.md:126`). Kam's `kam.kreiser` is `HPIPSC` (Administrator, B106 `:126`); the VM admin `hpsmadmin` signs in as Administrator (`B329_STATUS.md:69`). **Working hypothesis:** the Analyst role's "limited operations" excludes policy creation. Other gates you must also read: licences (SM showed `licensesAssigned 0 / available 10`, RELAYED `B329_STATUS.md:46`), Instant-On (`enableInstantOn false`), any read-only / maintenance state, the policy-template call, and the lockout / token state.

A. **Read-only diagnosis (VM reads via run-command; SM reads only as below).** (1) Local groups: members of `HPIPSC`, `HPIPSC_Analyst`, `HPIPSC_Operator`, `HPIPSC_Guest`, and the groups `paul.waite`, `kam.kreiser`, `hpsmadmin` belong to; account Enabled / locked-out / password-expired state (names and flags only). (2) SM's own role-to-permission definition: read the web client you already hold, `/Volumes/Laptop-DEV/!CODING/Datasec/HPSM-POC/.tools/b106/js/LS_app.min.js` (static, laptop), for the role names and the checks that hide or refuse "New Policy" / `POST api/v1/policies`; and SM's route list `1_Project_Definition/Briefs/B106_evidence/sm-client-api-routes.txt`. (3) SM's feature state that gates policy creation (licence, Instant-On, edition) from the Preferences / Licenses reads that SM's own dashboard load makes, counts and flags only. Write the root cause as a measured sentence, or "UNMEASURED: <what is missing>".
B. **Reproduce (after B329 is finished, see COLLISION RULES).** One sign-in as Paul (D below) BEFORE the fix, then the create attempt; record the exact refusal (status code, SM's words, whether the New Policy control is hidden or disabled). This is the control: the same attempt must fail before and succeed after.
C. **Fix: make Paul the highest-level SM user.** SM's highest role is Administrator = Windows local group **`HPIPSC`**. Add `paul.waite` to `HPIPSC` (`Add-LocalGroupMember`, via run-command). Record before / after membership of all four `HPIPSC*` groups (assert by program: exactly one membership added, no other group changed). **Do not** add him to the VM's local Administrators, Remote Desktop Users or any Azure role: SM Administrator is what Kam asked for and nothing beyond. Do not change his password, enabled state, lockout settings or any other account. Whether SM takes the highest or the first of several groups is UNMEASURED: after the add, sign in and read the role SM reports (`userRole`). If it still reports Analyst (or the create still fails), remove him from `HPIPSC_Analyst` (one change, record the undo `Add-LocalGroupMember -Group HPIPSC_Analyst -Member paul.waite`), and repeat. If that still fails, find the next gate from A; if the fix needs an SM setting, a licence-term change, a service restart, or a reset of Paul's password: **STOP and ask Tuesday** (a new password would have to reach Paul through Kam).
D. **Sign-in and create, as Paul, in-process only.** Credentials: `HPSM_SM_USER_PAUL` (`hpsm-sm\paul.waite`) and `HPSM_SM_USER_PAUL_PASSWORD` from the project's `4_Credentials/.env`; values read in-process, never printed, never written to a file that stays, never in a screenshot (password field empty in any capture). **Route (the one B161 and B329 proved):** headless Edge ON the VM against `https://localhost:7637/` (no password crosses the internet), run by a new scheduled task `B337-Paul` as the VM admin; the credential reaches it only through a file readable by SYSTEM and that user and deleted by its reader on first read; DevTools on VM-local port **9361** (free-check first with `netstat`; the laptop needs no port). Reuse the kit patterns by COPY (never edit): `/Volumes/Laptop-DEV/!CODING/Datasec/HPSM-POC/.tools/b161/` (`rcp.py`, `ps/10-12`) and, read-only, `.tools/b329/` for the redactor. If the editor will not take DOM events, call the page's own controller/service function (B329 brief "How you drive the editor"); only if both fail, an API call from the VM's localhost with Paul's token (`POST api/v1/signin`, then `POST api/v1/policies`) is accepted as a WEAKER proof: say so. Redact tokens, the password and any `pass|pwd|secret|key|token|credential|phrase` field before anything leaves the VM, as B329 does.
   - **Sign-in budget:** at most **4** sign-ins as Paul in the whole session (before-fix, after-fix, two spare). Lockout is 10 failures in 10 minutes for 10 minutes (RELAYED `B306_STATUS.md:53`). **A first failed sign-in: STOP** (never retry into a lockout of Paul's account).
   - **Paul may be signed in and working** (he has 8 devices / 5 tasks). Whether a second sign-in of the same account ends his session is UNMEASURED: record what you see; if SM tells you it will end another session, decline and STOP and ask. **His role change takes effect at his NEXT sign-in**, so Kam must tell him to log out and back in (put the one line in STATUS under WHAT KAM TELLS PAUL).
E. **Prove it.** As Paul, after the fix: (a) the role SM reports for him; (b) the Policies screen's New Policy control available; (c) create **one** policy named `ZZ-DATASEC-SYNTHETIC-B337 (test only, do not use)`, a harmless template / default values, **assigned to NOTHING** (no device, no group, no task, never "Set as Auto Remediation", never applied); decline any dialog that offers to assign, apply, assess or remediate and record it; (d) read it back (id, state, locked by, devices 0). **Counts:** read `AllDevicesCount`, the `Jobs` count and the `ScheduledReports` count BEFORE and AFTER (expected 8 / 5 / 1, quote what you read) and assert EQUAL; a change your actions could explain is a STOP, one they cannot explain (Paul working) is recorded, not acted on. Before the create, read once, counts and flags only, whether an auto-remediation policy or Instant-On / automatic-policy feature is ON; if either is ON, STOP and ask (RELAYED `B329_ANSWER-1` item 3). Also assert the other policies' metadata is equal before and after (name, id, validity, last modified, locked by; nothing else).
F. **Leave or delete the test policy.** Delete it only if B329 is finished, only through Paul's own session, only YOUR policy, and only if SM offers the delete (it is your own synthetic row, not Paul's work). Otherwise leave it and list it (name, id, state). Say which you did, and SM's behaviour. Never delete anything else; nothing on this laptop is removed (quarantine by rename).

